76,0$Basidiocarps distinctly pileate, nominally with no effused part; with thickened hyphae which may be interpreted as skeletals; causing white rot $Tyromyces

' Basidiocarps sessile to resupinate; hymenophore poroid; spores smooth spores, cystidia and hyphae negative in Melzer's reagent generative hyphae with clamps; tubes and context light-coloured cystidia absent; hyphal system di-or tri-mitic
77,0$Spores truncate to broadly ellipsoid, thick-walled $Perenniporia
77,78$Spores allantoid 10 ellipsoid, thin-walled
78,79$Dendroid binding hyphae present, skeletal hyphae absent
78,81$Dendroid binding hyphae absent, skeletal hyphae present, short tortuous binding hyphae present or absent
79,0$Basidiocarp resupinate to effused reflexed $Dichomitus
79,80$Basidiocarp pileate, sessile, fan shaped to dimidiate with a contracted base
80,0$Basidiocarp large and punky with papery smooth surface; causes a brown rot $Piptoporus
80,0$Basidiocarp small to medium, rather dense and hard when dry; causes a white rot $Polyporus
81,82$Basidiocarp pileate, sessile to dimidiate, woody hard to tough; pileus glabrous, with or without a distinct crust, often spreading from the base in reddish or black colours, or hirsute to tomentose; in some cases with a black layer between tomentum and context; all perennial species belong here
81,84$Basidiocarp resupinate to effused-reflexed or pileate and then with a narrow pileus; pileus glabrous to velutinate, never with a crust or black line under tomentum; all species annual
82,0$Basidiocarp soft and watery when fresh, mostly white to sordid brown when old or dried. short lived and annual; binding hyphae absent; skeletal hyphae relatively few in the context and generative hyphae dominate in the basidiocarps $Tyromyces


82,83$Basidiocarp woody hard to tough, perennial or annual, but long lived; pileus glabrous 10 hirsute, white, yellowish to sordid brown or black to reddish because of cuticle spreading from the base, binding hyphae present, but may he difficult to observe in some species; skeletal hyphae dominating in the basidiocarp
83,0$Basidiocarp tough to hard; annual species but long lived in the season; pileus white, greyish, sordid brown or blackish when dry, glabrous to hirsute, in .some species with a black line between the tomentum and the context; causing white rot $Trametes
83,0$Basidiocarp mostly woody hard to very tough, perennial; pileus white, sordid brown to black or reddish, sometimes with a cuticle spreading from the base, glabrous to finely velutinate, tomentum absent; causing brown rot $Fomitopsis
84,0$Skeletal hyphae in the dissepiments finely encrusted; species with a pale orange pore surface belong here $Skeletocutis
84,85$Skeletal hyphae mostly smooth, occasionally with scattered large crystals
85,0$Skeletal hyphae with a wide lumen totally dominating; generative hyphae often very difficult to find; basidia longitudinally septate; pore surface often discoloure to pale brown when dry; rare species $Protomerulius caryae
85,86$Skeletal hyphae thick-walled to solid ; generative hyphae normally easily observed; basidia not septate; pore surface variable; many common species
86,0$Causing a brown rot; all species with spores longer than 7 µm belong here $Antrodia
86,87$Causing a white rot
87,0$Spores ellipsoid to cylindric, up to 5 µm long; basidiocarps often semi translucent whitish when fresh, becoming dense and pale straw coloured when dry, pileate to resupinate; on dead wood or often on other fungi $Antrodiella
87,0$Spores allantoids to cylindric, 5-7 µm long; basidiocarps white, straw coloured to greyish and resupinate; always on dead wood $Diplomitoporus
